ge.\n\nOverall, the image provides a clear view of the ticket information and UI elements, but does not reveal any specific error messages or issues.\n\nStep 2: User located and lock confirmed\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → Users → Search bar\nSearch by employee ID. Confirm the account status badge shows Locked before continuing — do not reset a password on an account that isn't actually locked without further checks.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 2 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.80 / Medium]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a user's information displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is a web-based interface for managing users, tickets, and settings.\n    *   It has a dark blue color scheme with white text.\n    *   The top-left corner displays the CoreID logo, which is a stylized letter \"C\" made up of small squares.\n    *   Below the logo, there are several menu options, including \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n*   **User Information**\n    *   The user's name is SOL-04821, which is displayed in a light gray box.\n    *   Their role is J. Employee, which is also displayed in a light gray box.\n    *   There is a red button labeled \"Locked\" next to the user's information.\n*   **Error Message**\n    *   There is no error message visible in the screenshot.\n\nBased on the image, it appears that the user SOL-04821 is an employee with a locked account. The red button labeled \"Locked\" suggests that the account may be inactive or suspended. However, without more information, it is difficult to determine the exact reason for the lockout.\n\n**OBSERVED FACTS**\n\n*   The user's name is SOL-04821.\n*   The user's role is J. Employee.\n*   The account is locked.\n*   There is no error message visible.\n\n**INFERENCE**\n\n*   The account may be inactive or suspended due to the lockout.\n*   The user may need to contact an administrator to resolve the issue.\n\n**CONFIDENCE SCORE**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.8\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: Medium\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, but the exact reason for the lockout is unknown.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a white box in the center containing a list of options for identity verification. The purpose of the image is to provide a visual representation of the CoreID Admin Console and its features.\n\nHere are the details of the image:\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The top-left corner of the image has a dark blue bar with the words \"CoreID\" in white text, followed by \"Admin Console\" in smaller white text.\n\t+ Below this is a menu with the following options: \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n* **White Box**\n\t+ The white box is centered in the image and contains a list of options for identity verification.\n\t+ The title of the box is \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" in black text.\n\t+ Below the title are two checkboxes:\n\t\t- \"Employee ID matches ticket\"\n\t\t- \"Manager name matches ticket\"\n\t+ There is also a dropdown menu with the following options: \"Department matches record\", \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\", and \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n* **Background**\n\t+ The background of the image is a light gray color.\n\nOverall, the image provides a clear and concise visual representation of the CoreID Admin Console and its features, specifically the identity verification process. The use of a white box with black text and a light gray background makes the image easy to read and understand.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a login page for an application called \"CoreID\". The page has a white background with black text and a blue button.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The top-left corner of the page displays the text \"CoreID\" in white font, followed by \"Admin Console\" in smaller text below it.\n*   **Dashboard**\n    *   Below the \"CoreID Admin Console\" section, there is a menu item labeled \"Dashboard\".\n*   **Tickets**\n    *   Another menu item is labeled \"Tickets\".\n*   **Users**\n    *   The \"Users\" menu item is selected, as indicated by a dark gray background behind the text.\n*   **Reports**\n    *   A menu item labeled \"Reports\" is present.\n*   **Settings**\n    *   The final menu item is labeled \"Settings\".\n*   **Reset Password - J. Employee**\n    *   In the center of the page, a white box contains the text \"Reset Password - J. Employee\" in black font.\n    *   Below this, there is a temporary password field with the text \"Tq7#mVr2IkLp9x\" displayed.\n    *   A checkbox labeled \"Force change at next sign-in\" is also present.\n    *   A blue button with the text \"Generate Temporary Password\" is located below the checkbox.\n*   **Generate Temporary Password**\n    *   The blue button is highlighted, indicating that it is the current focus of the page.\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   There are no error messages visible on the page.\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a login page for an application called \"CoreID\" with a selected \"Users\" menu item. The page displays a temporary password field and a checkbox to force a password change at the next sign-in. A blue button is highlighted, allowing the user to generate a temporary password.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n*   The application is \"CoreID\".\n*   The current screen is the login page.\n*   The overall layout is a white background with black text and a blue button.\n*   The \"Users\" menu item is selected.\n*   The temporary password field contains the text \"Tq7#mVr2IkLp9x\".\n*   A checkbox is present to force a password change at the next sign-in.\n*   A blue button is highlighted to generate a temporary password.\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   None\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Software/application\n*   Severity: Medium\n*   Reason: The page is displaying a temporary password field and a checkbox to force a password change, indicating a potential issue with user authentication.\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The application is \"CoreID\".\n    *   The current screen is the login page.\n    *   The overall layout is a white background with black text and a blue button.\n    *   The \"Users\" menu item is selected.\n    *   The temporary password field contains the text \"Tq7#mVr2IkLp9x\".\n    *   A checkbox is present to force a password change at the next sign-in.\n    *   A blue button is highlighted to generate a temporary password.\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The user may have forgotten their password and is attempting to reset it.\n    *   The application may have a security feature to force a password change at the next sign-in.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, with no visible errors or warnings. The layout and design of the page are consistent with a typical login page.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket #SOL-84213 - Resolution**\n    *   The ticket number is SOL-84213.\n    *   The status is Resolved.\n*   **Resolution notes**\n    *   Identity verified (2 of 4 checks).\n    *   Temporary password issued via CoreID.\n    *   Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled.\n    *   No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n\nThe image provides clear information about the ticket resolution, including the ticket number, status, and resolution notes. The resolution notes indicate that the identity was verified, a temporary password was issued, and force-change-at-next-sign-in was enabled. Additionally, it is noted that no OTP was sent, and the reset was admin-mediated per policy. Overall, the image suggests that the ticket was resolved successfully, and the necessary actions were taken to secure the account.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Secur\n\n## PREVIOUS ATTEMPT ERRORS (MUST FIX ON THIS RETRY):\n- Scenario 3/4 Validation Error (Attempt 1): [\"Layer 1 Error: Step 1 has step_type='command' but command is empty.\", \"Layer 1 Error: Step 5 has step_type='command' but command is empty.\"]\n- Scenario 3/4 Validation Error (Attempt 2): [\"Layer 1 Error: Step 1 has step_type='tool_call' but 'platform'/'provider' is missing.\", \"Layer 1 Error: Step 5 has step_type='command' but command is empty.\"]\n\nAI: [{'name': 'get_platform_tools', 'args': {'platform_name': 'solandra'}, 'id': 'mWHLpKJ58', 'type': 'tool_call'}]\nTool: {\n  \"platform\": \"solandra\",\n  \"provider\": \"solandra\",\n  \"aliases\": [\n    \"coreid\",\n    \"coreid admin console\",\n    \"coreid_user_management\",\n    \"solandra group\",\n    \"solandra corp\",\n    \"solandra_ticketing\",\n    \"solandra_group\",\n    \"ticketing system\",\n    \"ticketing\"\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Solandra Corp IT Support platform covering CoreID Admin Console user lookup, identity verification, temporary password generation, and ticket resolution.\",\n  \"functions\": [\n    {\n      \"function_name\": \"search_user\",\n      \"description\": \"Searches the CoreID user directory by employee ID and returns the matching user's account stat